 This document describes the device data model parameters one should consider when creating a network services configuration template. For a wider picture, please see [](dev-device-features) or [](../devices.md).
 
-The services module currently supports two client-server services: **DNS** (fully implemented) and **syslog** (attribute schema defined, transformation logic ready, configuration templates not yet implemented).
+This fork adds a third: **SNMP** (agent only, implemented as a SERVER -- see the SNMP note in the user documentation). Upstream, the services module currently supports two client-server services: **DNS** (fully implemented) and **syslog** (attribute schema defined, transformation logic ready, configuration templates not yet implemented).
 
 **Notes:**
 
diff --git a/docs/module/services.md b/docs/module/services.md
index 6f1b212..acbbcb1 100644
--- a/docs/module/services.md
+++ b/docs/module/services.md
@@ -3,6 +3,8 @@
 
 The Network Services configuration module implements DNS and Syslog clients and servers; NTP and SNMP are planned for future releases.
 
+This *netlab* fork also implements an **SNMP agent** (`services.server.snmp`) -- see [SNMP agent](#services-snmp) below.
+
 ```eval_rst
 .. contents:: Table of Contents
    :depth: 2
@@ -70,3 +72,61 @@ The DNS server is enabled on supported nodes with the **services.server.dns** pa
 
 * Specify the Syslog server(s) on client nodes with the [common network services parameters](services-common-parameters)
 * The syslog server is enabled on supported nodes with the **services.server.syslog: True** parameter.
+
+(services-snmp)=
+## SNMP agent
+
+```{note}
+SNMP is a **platform extension**. Upstream *netlab* lists SNMP as planned for a
+future release; this fork implements the agent half now. Changes belong in
+`patches/netlab/`.
+```
+
+An SNMP agent is modelled as a **server**, not a client: it listens on UDP/161 and
+answers an NMS, exactly as a DNS server answers a resolver. It is therefore
+configured under `services.server.snmp`, and is deliberately absent from the
+module's `clients` list -- a node that only needs an `snmp-server community` line
+has no server to point at, and modelling it as a client would fail validation with
+*"is neither a client nor a server"*.
+
+Trap and inform *destinations* are the genuine client half of SNMP. They are not
+implemented yet and would arrive as `services.snmp.server` without disturbing this.
+
+| Operating system | SNMP agent |
+| ---------------- | :--: |
+| Arista EOS | ✅ |
+| Cisco IOS / IOS-XE | ✅ |
+
+Devices inheriting from `ios` (`iol`, `iosv`, `csr`, `cat8000v`, …) inherit the
+capability.
+
+### Parameters
+
+* `services.server.snmp.community` -- read-only community string. Defaults to `skyforge` (the community the collector's collectors poll with).
+* `services.server.snmp.location` -- optional `snmp-server location`.
+* `services.server.snmp.contact` -- optional `snmp-server contact`.
+
+Setting `services.server.snmp: True` enables the agent with the default community.
+
+### Example
+
+```yaml
+module: [ services ]
+
+nodes:
+  s1:
+    device: eos
+    services.server.snmp:
+      community: skyforge
+      location: lab-a
+```
+
+renders
+
+```
+snmp-server community skyforge ro
+snmp-server location lab-a
+```
+
+The access level is spelled per platform -- `ro` on EOS, `RO` on IOS -- because
+these strings are written to the device verbatim.
diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/services/arubacx.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/services/arubacx.j2
index 8a6ac80..db80f11 100644
--- a/netsim/ansible/templates/services/arubacx.j2
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/services/arubacx.j2
@@ -14,3 +14,13 @@ ip dns domain-list {{ services.dns.domain }}
 logging {{ syslog_host }}{%
    if services.syslog.transport_vrf is defined %} vrf {{ services.syslog.transport_vrf }}{% endif +%}
 {% endfor %}
+{% if services.server.snmp is defined %}
+{#
+    SNMP agent, ported VERBATIM from the snmp_config custom config module so the
+    generated configuration is byte-for-byte what this platform emitted before.
+    The only change is that the community is a parameter, defaulting to
+    `skyforge` -- the community the collector's collectors poll with. Any other default
+    leaves every device reachable and unpollable.
+#}
+snmp-server community {{ services.server.snmp.community|default('skyforge') }} ro
+{% endif %}
diff --git a/netsim/ansible/templates/services/asa.j2 b/netsim/ansible/templates/services/asa.j2
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1f73d30
--- /dev/null
+++ b/netsim/ansible/templates/services/asa.j2
@@ -0,0 +1,84 @@
+{% if services.server.snmp is defined %}
+{#
+    SNMP agent, ported VERBATIM from the snmp_config custom config module so the
+    generated configuration is byte-for-byte what this platform emitted before.
+    The only change is that the community is a parameter, defaulting to
+    `skyforge` -- the community the collector's collectors poll with. Any other default
+    leaves every device reachable and unpollable.
+#}
+snmp-server community {{ services.server.snmp.community|default('skyforge') }}
+{% endif %}
+{#
+  THE COLLECTOR IS REACHED OVER MANAGEMENT, and on an ASA every logging and
+  export destination is written in terms of an interface NAME, not an interface.
+  That name is `management`: netlab's own asav day-0 (netsim/install/libvirt/
+  asav/day0-config) sets `interface Management0/0 / nameif management`, and the
+  live demo-lab cdg-fw01 agrees -- `show nameif` reports Management0/0 -> management
+  while every data port keeps its own ifname. mgmt.ifname is "Management0/0",
+  which is the INTERFACE and not the name, so it deliberately is not used here.
+#}
+{% set _asa_mgmt_nameif = asa_mgmt_nameif|default('management') %}
+{% if services.syslog._server_list is defined %}
+{#
+  SYSLOG CLIENT. Measured 2026-09-11 on the live demo-lab cdg-fw01, with a
+  negative control so an empty answer could not be mistaken for an absent
+  feature:
+
+    show flarble wibble      -> ERROR: % Invalid input detected   (control)
+    show logging setting     -> "Syslog logging: disabled" and the full setting
+                                table                            -> client present
+    show running-config logging -> empty, no error                -> unconfigured
+
+  `logging enable` is the switch the setting table reports as disabled; without
+  it every other line here is inert configuration that reads as configured.
+#}
+logging enable
+logging timestamp
+logging trap informational
+{%   for _syslog_host in services.syslog._server_list[:4] %}
+logging host {{ _asa_mgmt_nameif }} {{ _syslog_host }}
+{%   endfor %}
+{% endif %}
+{% if services.flow.protocol|default('') == 'netflow' and services.flow._server_list is defined %}
+{#
+  NSEL, WHICH IS NOT CLASSIC NETFLOW AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS.
+
+  NetFlow Secure Event Logging exports NetFlow v9 records, so the wire protocol
+  and the collector's listener are the ones `netflow` already names -- but the
+  records are EVENTS on the firewall's connection table (created, torn down,
+  denied) and not a sampled or timed digest of forwarded packets. A firewall
+  does not sample; it knows every flow it permitted. That is why this block
+  declares netflow and NOT ipfix (the ASA has no IPFIX export) and NOT sflow
+  (`show sflow` does not exist here), and why it asks for no sampler: there is
+  no packet sampling to configure, and `services.flow.sampling` is deliberately
+  NOT declared on this device so the module leaves it alone rather than the
+  template pretending to honour it.
+
+  MEASURED 2026-09-11 on the live demo-lab cdg-fw01 (ASA 9.24(1)), against the
+  same `show flarble wibble` control that errors:
+
+    show flow-export counters       -> empty, no error   -> NSEL present
+    show running-config flow-export -> empty, no error   -> unconfigured
+
+  IT MUST JOIN global_policy, NOT ADD A POLICY. An ASA permits exactly ONE
+  global service-policy and this one already has it -- `show running-config
+  service-policy` on cdg-fw01 returns `service-policy global_policy global`, and
+  a second `service-policy ... global` is refused. So the export class is added
+  to the policy that is already applied, which is also the form Cisco documents.
+#}
+{%   for _flow_host in services.flow._server_list[:2] %}
+flow-export destination {{ _asa_mgmt_nameif }} {{ _flow_host }} {{ services.flow.port|default(2055) }}
+{%   endfor %}
+flow-export template timeout-rate 1
+flow-export active refresh-interval 1
+!
+class-map skyforge-flow-export
+ match any
+!
+policy-map global_policy
+ class skyforge-flow-export
+{%   for _flow_host in services.flow._server_list[:2] %}
+  flow-export event-type all destination {{ _flow_host }}
+{%   endfor %}
+!
+{% endif %}
